# PowerShell scripts must reach Windows with CRLF endings. They are also stored
# UTF-8 *with BOM* (see below) -- the BOM is file content, so git preserves it.
#
# Why the BOM matters: Windows PowerShell 5.1 decodes .ps1 as ANSI (CP1252)
# unless a UTF-8 BOM is present. A UTF-8 em dash then arrives as three CP1252
# characters, the last of which (0x94) is a smart double quote -- and PowerShell
# honours smart quotes as string delimiters, so the file stops parsing with
# misleading "Missing closing '}'" errors far from the real cause.
#
# Keep .ps1 sources ASCII-only as a second line of defence.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts must stay LF or they fail on macOS/Linux with bad interpreter.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binary assets: never touch line endings.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
